  "theory": {
    "intro": "$\\sin^{2}nx$ and $\\cos^{2}nx$ have no primitive you can write down directly, so first rewrite them using a double angle identity. That turns the square into $\\cos 2nx$, which integrates in one step.",
    "facts": [
      "From $\\cos 2A = 1 - 2\\sin^{2}A$ comes $\\sin^{2}nx = \\dfrac{1 - \\cos 2nx}{2}$.",
      "From $\\cos 2A = 2\\cos^{2}A - 1$ comes $\\cos^{2}nx = \\dfrac{1 + \\cos 2nx}{2}$.",
      "The angle doubles, so squaring $\\sin 5x$ produces $\\cos 10x$, not $\\cos 5x$.",
      "$\\int \\sin^{2}nx\\,dx = \\dfrac{x}{2} - \\dfrac{\\sin 2nx}{4n} + C$",
      "$\\int \\cos^{2}nx\\,dx = \\dfrac{x}{2} + \\dfrac{\\sin 2nx}{4n} + C$",
      "The denominator is $4n$ because the $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ from the identity multiplies the $\\dfrac{1}{2n}$ from integrating $\\cos 2nx$.",
      "The two results differ only in the sign of the $\\sin 2nx$ term.",
      "The rule holds for fractional $n$ as well, so $n = \\dfrac{1}{4}$ gives a denominator of $1$.",
      "Work in radians, and for a definite integral substitute the limits without a constant of integration.",
      "Check any result by differentiating, since $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(\\dfrac{\\sin 2nx}{4n}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\cos 2nx}{2}$."
    ],
    "steps": [
      "Write the square using the double angle identity for $\\cos 2nx$.",
      "Take the factor $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ outside the integral.",
      "Integrate term by term, dividing the $\\cos 2nx$ term by $2n$.",
      "Substitute the limits, for a definite integral."
    ]
  },
